#!/usr/bin/env python3
# GENERATED by scripts/gen_paths.py from references/paths.toml and
# scripts/lib/paths-templates/broomva_paths.py.tmpl. Do not edit: change those
# and run `python3 scripts/gen_paths.py`.
"""Where Broomva state lives: the path registry, embedded (BRO-3216).

Three scopes (unified-layout ADR, decision 1):

- repo:      <git top level of the target>/.broomva/<path>, legacy .control/<path>
- workspace: the repo names under the governing workspace, $BROOMVA_WORKSPACE
             (then MAESTRO_WORKSPACE, then the deprecated BSTACK_WORKSPACE), ~/broomva
- machine:   $BROOMVA_HOME/<path>, default ~/.broomva

Migration rule, on disk: a name resolves to its new location when that exists;
otherwise to the legacy one when that exists, or when the repo still has its
legacy root (it has not moved yet, so a writer must not split the ledger);
otherwise to the new one. Inside a git tree (``tree_path``): the new path when
the tree holds it, else the legacy path, else None. A tree that cannot be read
(an unknown revision, no git, not a repo) raises TreeReadError: "absent" and
"could not look" are never the same answer, because a gate reading "no signers
file" must not get it from a failed git call.

Containment, not provenance. A path these readers return lies inside the repo:
`.broomva/` and `.control/` must be real folders, and the entry a name resolves
to must not be a symlink (repo paths are one level deep, so that is every
component); a committed symlink in a tree is refused the same way (LayoutError).
An owner-written file must also have a single link: a hard link elsewhere in
the repo lets a write to an ungoverned name change it, past every lexical gate,
and git cannot record the alias for Owner Signature to see. That check sees only
a link that still exists (one made, written through and removed leaves nothing),
any process on the volume can trip it by linking the file (backup tools that
hard-link do), and files inside a folder entry (hooks/) are not counted. An entry
that is neither a regular file nor a folder (a FIFO, socket or device) is refused. They cannot tell who
wrote a file: a `.broomva/policy.yaml` an agent created with `cp -r` reads like
the owner's. Owner-written governance is protected where
it is written, by the gates (literal lists, OWNER_PATHS) and Owner Signature
(ADR decision 2: gates never consult the registry), and doctor reports a name
under both roots after the fact.

Two limits, stated: containment holds when the reader checks, and a folder
swapped for a symlink afterwards is followed by the caller's later open (open
with O_NOFOLLOW where that matters); and machine-scope names have no
containment check: $BROOMVA_HOME is the user's own.

A repo root is never searched for by name and never falls back to the cwd: it is
the git top level of the path a tool operates on, or of $CLAUDE_PROJECT_DIR when
the tool passes none (None or ""), and none at all when $HOME is that top level.
git runs with GIT_DIR and GIT_WORK_TREE removed, so a caller inside a git hook
or a rebase --exec still gets the target's repo.

Adding a name: add a line to references/paths.toml and run
`python3 scripts/gen_paths.py` (it needs Python 3.11 for tomllib; this reader
runs on 3.9). Python 3.9+, stdlib only. CLI: ``broomva_paths.py --help``.
"""

